  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 130 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nThe main focus is a complex storm system taking shape mid to \nlate week, potentially impacting Thanksgiving holiday travel\nespecially east of the Mississippi River and through the \neastern CONUS. \n\nOverall, confidence is increasing on the phasing of northern \nand southern stream waves owing to a stronger and wetter system \nevolving from the Midwest to the East Coast during this time. \nWhat is uncertain is when/where this phasing will occur, which \nwill dictate the storm track, where the heavier precipitation \namounts will occur, and even thermal parameters and\nprecipitation type (rain versus snow). Moisture from the \nsouthern stream wave could get caught up into the flow around \nthe digging northern stream low over the Midwest leading to \npotentially an axis of heavier precipitation in the vicinity of \nan inverted surface trough initially Wednesday night, and then \nas the phasing occurs and the system deepens additional moisture\nin the wrap-around or deformation could be thrust back into the\nMidwest maintaining bouts of heavier precipitation through \nThanksgiving night. Not surprisingly given the complex nature of\nthe system the forecast ranges from dry or rain with little/no \nsnow to potentially heavy wet snow of 6+ inches aided by a \nstronger system and dynamic cooling across parts of the area. \nLatest probabilistic WSSI has trended a bit higher and spatially\na bigger footprint with probabilities for minor impacts at\n10-30%, and have also introduced 5-20% probabilities for moderate \nimpacts focused across parts of the south/east service area for\n12z Thursday to 12z Friday. Still, considerable uncertainty exists\non some of the finer details i.e. precipitation type, amounts and\nlocation due to the complex interaction of these waves. Given the\nbusy holiday travel period, please stay tuned for updates, \nespecially if you are planning any travel from the Midwest to the\nEast Coast. I want to stress to also seek out trusted weather \nsources for these updates. Any source showing model snow amounts\nat this range should not be trusted, as they don't take into account\nfactors such as ground and pavement temperatures, wet-bulb \ntemperatures aloft (which some models show warming as much as 1-2\ndegrees Celsius) which will play pivotal in precipitation types,\nnot to mention that most of these are also using a 10:1 snow-liquid \nratio that will most likely not occur.",
